<!-- ARTICLE BODY -->
<section style="background:var(--tw-white);padding:2.5rem 0 4rem;">
  <div class="container" style="max-width:1040px;">

    <!-- INTRO CALLOUT -->
    <div class="tw-fade" style="background:var(--tw-ivory);border-left:4px solid var(--tw-gold);border-radius:0 12px 12px 0;padding:1.75rem 2rem;margin-bottom:2.5rem;">
      <p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.05rem;font-style:italic;color:var(--tw-forest);line-height:1.9;margin:0;">Some of what we carry does not belong to our own stories. It belongs to the stories of those who came before us, stories that were never fully told, never fully grieved, and never fully healed. Understanding this is not about assigning blame. It is about finding freedom.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tw-fade" style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.05rem;line-height:1.95;color:var(--tw-text-dark);">

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">What Intergenerational Trauma Actually Means</h2>
      <p>Intergenerational trauma, sometimes called transgenerational or generational trauma, refers to the way that the psychological and emotional impact of a traumatic experience does not stay contained within the person who lived through it. Instead, it ripples outward across time, shaping the inner world of children and grandchildren who may have had no direct exposure to the original event at all.</p>
      <p>This transmission happens through several channels. It happens through behavior: a parent who survived chaos may raise children in rigid control, or in anxious hypervigilance, passing on the nervous system responses the trauma taught them without ever naming what they are doing or why. It happens through silence: families that cannot speak about what happened leave their descendants to make meaning of grief and fear with no context to understand it. And according to a growing body of research, it may also happen through biology, with trauma leaving measurable marks on the way genes are expressed that can be passed from parent to child.</p>
      <p>According to <a href="https://www.psychologytoday.com/ca/basics/intergenerational-trauma" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--tw-gold);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);">Psychology Today Canada</a>, even young children detect and react to their parent's anxiety cues. The worldview that trauma creates in a person, the sense that the world is dangerous, that love is unreliable, that survival requires constant vigilance, can be absorbed by children long before they have the language to name what they are absorbing.</p>

      <!-- PULL QUOTE -->
      <div style="margin:3rem 0;padding:2rem;border-top:2px solid var(--tw-gold);border-bottom:2px solid var(--tw-gold);text-align:center;">
        <p style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:1.65rem;font-style:italic;color:var(--tw-forest);line-height:1.4;margin:0;">"Some of what we feel as our own anxiety, our own shame, our own fear of abandonment, may actually be an inheritance. Recognizing that does not diminish the experience. It changes what healing needs to address."</p>
      </div>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">Where It Comes From</h2>
      <p>Intergenerational trauma can originate from many kinds of experiences. War and displacement. Poverty and economic precarity sustained across generations. Racial and cultural oppression. Immigration and the profound losses of language, community, and belonging that so often accompany it. Domestic violence, addiction, and abuse within family systems. The experience of growing up with a parent who was themselves deeply wounded and had no tools to heal.</p>
      <p>In the Canadian context, intergenerational trauma is particularly visible in the experience of Indigenous communities, for whom the legacies of residential schools, forced removal from land, and systematic cultural suppression continue to shape mental health outcomes across generations. Research has consistently documented how the cumulative impact of these policies extends into the lives of people who were never directly subjected to them.</p>
      <p>But intergenerational trauma is not confined to any particular culture or community. It lives in families where a grandparent survived a famine and never stopped hoarding food. In families where a parent witnessed violence as a child and learned that love comes with danger. In families where generations of unspoken grief were managed through emotional distance, and where that distance came to feel like what family simply is.</p>

      <!-- KEY INSIGHT BOX -->
      <div style="background:linear-gradient(135deg,rgba(44,57,48,0.05) 0%,rgba(162,123,92,0.08) 100%);border:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.2);border-radius:16px;padding:1.75rem 2rem;margin:2.5rem 0;">
        <p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--tw-gold);margin-bottom:0.75rem;">Key Insight</p>
        <p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.97rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0;line-height:1.85;">Research in epigenetics, the study of how experience changes the way genes are expressed, has found that trauma can alter gene expression in ways that affect stress response systems, and that some of these changes can be inherited. Studies of Holocaust survivors and their descendants, as well as research into communities affected by famine and war, have found measurable biological differences in stress hormones and immune function across generations. The body remembers what the mind was never told.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">Signs You May Be Carrying Intergenerational Trauma</h2>
      <p>One of the things that makes intergenerational trauma so difficult to identify is that it does not announce itself as inherited. It presents as your own anxiety, your own relationship difficulties, your own chronic sense of not being enough. It can take a long time to recognize that some of what you carry has roots that go further back than your own life.</p>

      <!-- SIGNS LIST -->
      <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1rem;margin:1.5rem 0 2.5rem;">
        <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;align-items:flex-start;padding:1.25rem;background:var(--tw-ivory);border-radius:12px;">
          <div style="width:36px;height:36px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--tw-forest);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;"><i class="bi bi-exclamation-triangle" style="color:var(--tw-gold);font-size:0.9rem;"></i></div>
          <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-weight:600;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0 0 0.3rem;font-size:0.95rem;">Anxiety or hypervigilance without a clear source</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.9rem;margin:0;color:var(--tw-text-mid);line-height:1.7;">A persistent sense of threat, of needing to be on guard, of waiting for something bad to happen, even when your life is objectively safe. A nervous system that behaves as though the danger your ancestors faced is still present.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;align-items:flex-start;padding:1.25rem;background:var(--tw-ivory);border-radius:12px;">
          <div style="width:36px;height:36px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--tw-forest);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;"><i class="bi bi-chat-square-x" style="color:var(--tw-gold);font-size:0.9rem;"></i></div>
          <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-weight:600;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0 0 0.3rem;font-size:0.95rem;">Difficulty talking about emotions in your family</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.9rem;margin:0;color:var(--tw-text-mid);line-height:1.7;">Families affected by intergenerational trauma often develop rules, spoken or unspoken, about which feelings are allowed and which must be suppressed. Grief, anger, and vulnerability are frequently the first to be shut out.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;align-items:flex-start;padding:1.25rem;background:var(--tw-ivory);border-radius:12px;">
          <div style="width:36px;height:36px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--tw-forest);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;"><i class="bi bi-repeat" style="color:var(--tw-gold);font-size:0.9rem;"></i></div>
          <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-weight:600;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0 0 0.3rem;font-size:0.95rem;">Repeating patterns across generations</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.9rem;margin:0;color:var(--tw-text-mid);line-height:1.7;">Noticing that the same dynamics, the same relationship difficulties, the same coping strategies, appear in your life as they did in your parents' lives and perhaps your grandparents' as well. Repetition is one of trauma's clearest signatures.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;align-items:flex-start;padding:1.25rem;background:var(--tw-ivory);border-radius:12px;">
          <div style="width:36px;height:36px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--tw-forest);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;"><i class="bi bi-person-x" style="color:var(--tw-gold);font-size:0.9rem;"></i></div>
          <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-weight:600;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0 0 0.3rem;font-size:0.95rem;">Deep shame or unworthiness with no clear cause</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.9rem;margin:0;color:var(--tw-text-mid);line-height:1.7;">A pervasive sense of being fundamentally flawed, not good enough, or undeserving of good things, that does not seem to connect logically to your own experiences. Shame travels particularly well across generations, often disguised as personality.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div style="display:flex;gap:1.25rem;align-items:flex-start;padding:1.25rem;background:var(--tw-ivory);border-radius:12px;">
          <div style="width:36px;height:36px;border-radius:50%;background:var(--tw-forest);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;"><i class="bi bi-person-heart" style="color:var(--tw-gold);font-size:0.9rem;"></i></div>
          <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-weight:600;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:0 0 0.3rem;font-size:0.95rem;">Complicated relationships with your parents or family</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.9rem;margin:0;color:var(--tw-text-mid);line-height:1.7;">Loving someone who was themselves wounded in ways they never addressed, and who passed that wounding to you in ways neither of you fully understood, creates a particular kind of grief. Complicated, layered, and often without the validation of an obvious explanation.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">This Is Not About Blaming Your Parents</h2>
      <p>One of the most important things to hold onto when exploring intergenerational trauma is that understanding it is not the same as assigning blame. Your parents, like you, were shaped by forces that were often beyond their control. They parented from the resources available to them, resources that were themselves limited by what had been done to them and what had never been healed.</p>
      <p>Understanding that your father's emotional unavailability may have been rooted in his own childhood experience of not being seen does not mean you pretend it did not affect you. It means you can grieve what you did not receive without needing to make him a villain to justify that grief. It means you can hold both things at once: compassion for what he carried, and honest acknowledgment of what that cost you.</p>
      <p>This is one of the most challenging pieces of work in therapy. And it is also, for many people, one of the most liberating. Because when you stop needing someone to be entirely at fault, you also stop needing to carry the full weight of the story yourself.</p>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">How Therapy Helps With Intergenerational Trauma</h2>
      <p>Working with intergenerational trauma in therapy is not primarily about recovering specific memories or reconstructing a detailed history of your family. It is about understanding the patterns that have shaped you, making sense of responses that may have seemed irrational or inexplicable, and beginning to develop a different relationship with what you have inherited.</p>
      <p>Good therapy for intergenerational trauma holds several things at once. It validates your experience without requiring you to minimize what happened to you. It introduces your family's story as context without using it as an excuse. It helps you locate yourself within a larger narrative while also affirming that you are not simply a product of that narrative and that you have the capacity to respond differently than what was modeled for you.</p>
      <p>Trauma-informed care is especially important in this work. A trauma-informed therapist understands that the nervous system responses you carry may be deeply old, learned in environments where they genuinely kept you safer, and that they will not simply vanish because you understand their origin intellectually. Change happens through felt experience, through the body slowly learning that the original threat is no longer present, and through enough safety to begin processing what has been held for so long.</p>
      <p>Narrative approaches to therapy can also be powerful here, helping you examine and begin to reauthor the story of your family in ways that include your own agency and your own future. The goal is not to erase the story. It is to stop being entirely written by it.</p>
      <p>If you are also navigating anxiety that feels disconnected from your present circumstances, our article on <a href="/blog/understanding-anxiety" style="color:var(--tw-gold);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);">understanding anxiety and the nervous system</a> may offer some useful context alongside this one. And if you recognize burnout as part of what you are carrying, the piece on <a href="/blog/burnout-is-not-laziness" style="color:var(--tw-gold);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);">burnout and chronic depletion</a> speaks to the way long-term stress accumulates in the body in ways that often have roots deeper than the present moment.</p>

      <!-- WHAT YOU CAN DO BOX -->
      <div style="background:var(--tw-forest);border-radius:16px;padding:2.5rem;margin:3rem 0;">
        <p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.72rem;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:0.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--tw-gold);margin-bottom:1.25rem;">Where to Begin</p>
        <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:1.25rem;">
          <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;">
            <div style="width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;background:rgba(162,123,92,0.2);border:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:600;font-size:0.9rem;">1</div>
            <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--tw-cream);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Get curious about your family history</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(220,215,201,0.7);margin:0;line-height:1.7;">Not to excavate blame, but to build context. What did your parents or grandparents live through? What was never spoken about? What patterns do you notice repeating? Curiosity, rather than judgment, is the starting point.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;">
            <div style="width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;background:rgba(162,123,92,0.2);border:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:600;font-size:0.9rem;">2</div>
            <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--tw-cream);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Notice your body's responses</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(220,215,201,0.7);margin:0;line-height:1.7;">Intergenerational trauma often lives in physical sensation before it has words. Noticing where tension, contraction, or hyperarousal appear in your body, and beginning to get curious about what they might be responding to, is meaningful work.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;">
            <div style="width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;background:rgba(162,123,92,0.2);border:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:600;font-size:0.9rem;">3</div>
            <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--tw-cream);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Practice breaking the silence</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(220,215,201,0.7);margin:0;line-height:1.7;">Intergenerational trauma is sustained partly by the silence around it. Beginning to name what you notice, to a trusted person, a therapist, or even in writing, begins to interrupt the transmission. You do not have to have the full story to start.</p></div>
          </div>
          <div style="display:flex;gap:1rem;align-items:flex-start;">
            <div style="width:28px;height:28px;border-radius:50%;background:rgba(162,123,92,0.2);border:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;color:var(--tw-gold);font-weight:600;font-size:0.9rem;">4</div>
            <div><p style="font-family:'DM Sans',sans-serif;font-size:0.85rem;font-weight:500;color:var(--tw-cream);margin:0 0 0.2rem;">Seek a therapist who understands cultural and family systems</p><p style="font-family:'Lora',Georgia,serif;font-size:0.85rem;color:rgba(220,215,201,0.7);margin:0;line-height:1.7;">Intergenerational trauma work benefits enormously from a therapist who understands your cultural context and does not require you to spend your sessions explaining your background before you can begin addressing what brought you there.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 style="font-family:'Cormorant Garamond',Georgia,serif;font-size:2rem;color:var(--tw-forest);margin:2.5rem 0 1rem;padding-bottom:0.5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(44,57,48,0.1);">Breaking the Cycle</h2>
      <p>One of the most powerful motivations people bring to this work is the desire to stop the cycle. To not pass what they carry to their children. To be the generation in which the pattern ends.</p>
      <p>This is a profound and worthy goal. And it is worth knowing that you do not have to be perfectly healed to begin interrupting the transmission. The act of becoming conscious of the pattern, of naming it, of choosing even once to respond differently than your nervous system's first instinct, is itself a break in the chain. Healing is not all or nothing. It happens in increments, and each increment matters.</p>
      <p>The research is clear that therapeutic intervention can interrupt intergenerational trauma transmission. Children of parents who receive support for their own trauma show significantly different outcomes than children of parents who do not. Healing yourself is an act that extends beyond your own lifetime. It reaches forward into the lives of those who come after you, and backward in a kind of retroactive compassion toward those who came before.</p>
      <p>If you are ready to begin exploring what you are carrying, and what might be possible if you did not have to carry it alone, Shimul offers a <a href="https://twishcare.myndweb.app/shimul/booking" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--tw-gold);text-decoration:none;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(162,123,92,0.4);">free 15-minute consultation</a> with no pressure and no obligation.</p>

